WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.300
Governments run many
different public services.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.600
Each one is often built from scratch.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.400
Registry Stack is open-source software that lets
public authorities build them from the same few parts.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.800
Records and registries. Steps and
roles. Signed answers and certificates.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.400
Each service simply uses a different mix.

NOTE The same definitions

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.700
They can use the same definitions, too.
PublicSchema publishes them openly.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.500
Each registry copies the definitions
it needs, like Person or Household,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.900
and adds its own: an enrolment,
a farm holding, a birth.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.500
After that, no registry looks
PublicSchema up while it runs.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:47.500
So a signed answer about a Person
means the same thing on both sides.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.900
Separate records. The same definitions.

NOTE One request

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.600
Now, a licence renewal.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.900
Submitted. Then a check: another
authority sends a signed answer.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.300
A question goes back to the
requester: waiting for you.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.900
Their reply comes in, and
the request is approved.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.500
The renewal is recorded,
and a notice goes out.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.500
Other services reuse the same steps.

NOTE Answers out

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.500
A business registers. Its record
changes, and stays where it is.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.500
A sealed certificate comes off the record.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.700
Anyone checking it gets a signed yes.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.200
Systems get an answer, not a copy.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:33.100
Other authorities ask too: a city council,
a ministry. Each gets only a signed answer.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.500
Records stay put. Only
signed answers travel.

NOTE Own installations

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.500
Each authority runs its own installation:

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.700
its own registries, an engine that runs the
steps, sign-in, roles and an audit trail,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.700
all on its own servers.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.300
Step back: many installations,
the same parts.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.100
One can keep several registries.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.500
They exchange signed answers
directly, never copies.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.100
Two share government servers,
but not their records.

NOTE Templates

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.500
One council’s service can
become another’s template.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.900
Each council adapts it: one adds
a site visit, one drops the fee.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.900
The design travels. The records never do.

NOTE Close

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.100
Many public services,
built from the same parts.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.100
Each authority keeps its own records.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.900
Registry Stack. Open source,
at registrystack.org.
